Warzone 2100 2.3.9 for RISC OS (release 2.3.9-12)
=================================================

Warzone 2100 is a real-time strategy game, originally released by Pumpkin
Studios in 1999 and developed since 2004 as free software by the Warzone
2100 Project. This is the 2.3.9 release (2012), the last one that draws
everything with fixed-function OpenGL, ported to RISC OS.

Tested on a Raspberry Pi 4: playable in a 1024x768 desktop window, with
sound and music.


Requirements
------------
- RISC OS 5 on an ARMv7 machine with VFP: a Raspberry Pi 2 or later,
  or a Cortex-A8/A9 board such as the BeagleBoard-xM, PandaBoard, ARMini
  or an i.MX6 board. A Pi 4 is recommended: everything is drawn in
  software, so slower machines need a small window (640x480).
- SharedUnixLibrary 1.16 or later and ARMEABISupport (both from PackMan).
- The PThreadTicker module is included, and !Run loads it (or a newer
  copy from !System). It runs the code that switches between the game's
  threads, so it's paged in whichever program is running.
- About 32MB of free memory for the program, plus the game's heap, which
  goes in a dynamic area called "Warzone2100 Heap".
- For sound: SharedSoundBuffer, StreamManager and SharedSound. Without
  them the game runs silently. SharedSound is part of RISC OS (in !System
  on most machines). SharedSoundBuffer and StreamManager are John
  Duffell's freeware: download ssb.zip from Andrew Sellors' RDPClient page
  and merge its !System into yours:

    https://orac.co.uk/software/rdpclient/rdpclient.html

  John Duffell's own site (now on the Internet Archive) has more details:

    https://web.archive.org/web/20110920080106/http://www.duffell.riscos.me.uk/


Running
-------
Double-click !Warzone2100. The game opens a 1024x768 desktop window
(800x600 or 640x480 on a smaller screen).
- Options > Video changes the size and switches to full screen; the
  change takes effect the next time the game starts.
- Settings, saved games and screenshots are kept in
  <Choices$Write>.Warzone2100.
- Errors always go to <Wimp$ScrapDir>.Warzone2100log, which is empty
  after a normal run. If the game doesn't start or stops unexpectedly,
  send that file with your report.
- For a full log, turn logging on in !Run (remove the | from
  "| Set Warzone2100$Log 1", or *Set Warzone2100$Log 1 before running
  the game) and repeat the problem. The game's own log then also goes to
  <Choices$Write>.Warzone2100.WZlog, and a line of thread-switcher
  counters to <Wimp$ScrapDir>.WZTickerStats when the game quits. Send
  those too.
- If the game doesn't start at all, run the checker: open !Warzone2100
  (Shift+double-click) and double-click Check. It goes through what the
  game does before its window opens, one step at a time, in a window,
  and writes the same report to <Wimp$ScrapDir>.WZCheck. If it stops
  part way, the last line shows where. Send WZCheck with your report.


Things to know
--------------
- Everything is drawn in software (riscos-mesa, a build of Mesa 3D for
  RISC OS). Keep the window small for a good frame rate; 640x480 is the
  game's smallest size and the quickest.
- Or render smaller than the window: set Warzone2100$RenderSize (e.g.
  640x480 or 800x600; see "Render size" in !Run) and the picture is
  stretched to the window or the full screen, so a big window or full
  screen costs no more than the render size.
- On a Raspberry Pi with the VideoOverlay module, the game shows its
  frames with the hardware overlay instead of plotting them: quicker, and
  no tearing. It goes back to plotting while a menu or window is over the
  game. With the overlay the game renders at 800x600 (640x480 on screens
  up to 1024x768), stretched to the window or screen for free.
  Warzone2100$RenderSize (in !Run) picks another size, or "off" for the
  window's own size; Warzone2100$Overlay 0 turns the overlay off.
- With a render size, full screen (Alt+Return) is a "full window": a
  borderless window over the whole screen, with no mode change. Other
  programs keep running, and the icon bar pops up at the bottom of the
  screen; click the game to bring it back to the front.
  Warzone2100$FullWindow 0 gives the old full screen.
- Texture filtering is cheaper than on other systems (bilinear, no
  anisotropic filtering). For more speed use unfiltered textures, for
  better quality the usual ones: see "Texture filtering" in !Run
  (Warzone2100$Filter nearest / smooth / best / fast). The game keeps the
  last choice in its Choices.
- To save time, a new configuration starts with shadows off, vsync off
  and fog set to "Fog Of War" instead of "Mist". Alt+S turns shadows on;
  shadows and fog are in Options > Graphics, vsync in Options > Video.
  Each costs around 10% of the frame rate.
- The game has an icon on the icon bar. Quit from its menu, or the
  window's close icon, quits the game (your settings are saved first); so
  does a desktop shutdown.
- Sound and music play through SharedSoundBuffer (with StreamManager and
  SharedSound, in !System on most machines); without them the game is
  silent. Volume is in Options > Sound. Sound costs a little speed: to
  turn it off, set sound=0 in <Choices$Write>.Warzone2100.config.
- The campaign videos (sequences.wz) are a separate download and are
  not included. The game runs without them.
- Multiplayer over the internet used a lobby server that no longer exists.
  Games on a local network, or joining a host by address, may work.


Credits and licences
--------------------
Warzone 2100: the Warzone 2100 Project (wz2100.net). GPL version 2 or
later; see docs.COPYING and docs.COPYING-README.

This build contains, statically linked:
- riscos-mesa: Mesa 3D 20.3.5 (MIT), GLU and SDL 2.26 (zlib licence) with
  their RISC OS drivers; see docs.riscos-mesa-LICENCES.
- QuesoGLC 0.7.2 (LGPL 2.1), FreeType 2.10.1 (FreeType licence),
  fontconfig 2.14.1 and expat 2.2.9 (MIT), PhysicsFS 2.0.3 and zlib
  (zlib licence), libpng 1.6.37 (libpng licence), popt 1.16 (MIT),
  libogg 1.3.5, libvorbis 1.3.7 and libtheora 1.1.1 (BSD).
- UnixLib 5.0.3.1 (riscos-unixlib: GCCSDK's UnixLib with the ports' fixes)
  and the GCC runtime from GCCSDK.

Fonts: with PackMan's fontconfig installed (UnixFC), the game uses its
setup - the system's fonts and font cache - like other fontconfig
programs, and adds its own DejaVu Sans 2.37 (see docs.DejaVu-LICENSE).
Without it, the game uses only its own fonts and fontconfig setup, so
PackMan's fontconfig isn't needed.

The RISC OS changes are in docs.patches: they apply to the upstream
Warzone 2100 2.3.9, QuesoGLC 0.7.2 and PhysicsFS 2.0.3 sources.
